  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   - Use a multi-phased optimization strategy combining Bayesian optimization with constraints for continuous search, followed by discrete repair.\n   - Integrate five gradient-based local optimizers targeting both improvement and repair feasibility.\n   - Phase I: Gaussian Process regression with Expected Improvement acquisition guides exploration, balancing global search and reuse of good candidates.\n   - Phase II: For top candidates, apply a curated set of nonlinear transformations that respect integral constraints, using gradient information from the last evaluation.\n   - Address: The parent solution's slow convergence near optimum points. This approach evolves from global Bayesian to targeted local optimization with constraints enforced via repair.\n   - Bottleneck: Original optimization's heavy repair function. Our solution passes gradient information to secondary optimizers.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - h \u2208 [0,1]\u00b2 schedule with \u222bh dx = 1\n   - Four-point hybrid objective combining integral preservation and gradient alignment\n   - Repair function: Inconstrained L-BFGS-B optimizer with constraint handling component\n   - Communication: Gradient arguments propagating from objective evaluations to repair phase\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Initialization: Require valid initial sample (if available)\n   - Objective:\n     - Takes h_vec \u2208 R\u207f\u207a\n     - Returns ( -value, value,)\n     - Performs global-to-local gradient assessment\n   - Repair:\n     - Input: repair_center, repair_candidates, repair_optimizer\n     - Technique: Gradient-assisted constrained optimization\n     - Output: Valid h_vec preserving integral constraint\n   - Optimization:\n     - Phase I: Optimize using fmin_cobyla with repair checks\n     - Phase II: Apply curvature-based transformations with integral repair\n     - Success condition: Maintain stable integral over multiple phases\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Primary: Expected Improvement regression guiding shifts\n   - Secondary: Transformation-guided descent with repair kernels\n   - Early stopping: After three consecutive failures to improve global minimum\n   - Transformations built to reduce function oscillations at boundaries\n   - Repair kernels: Adaptive gradient projection for integral preservation\n\n5. Budget Estimation:\n   - CPU time: 50% to repair phase, 50% to optimization\n   - Objective evaluations: 40-100 per candidate depending on convergence\n   - Typical run length: 20-50 iterations against budget\n   - Critical point: Repair phase prevents wasted evaluations\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Constraints: Integral exactly 1 (within 1e-6) on return\n   - Objective bounds: -1.0 to 0.38 recorded\n   - Final check: Verify repair candidate via integral re-evaluation\n   - Return: (h_vec, -C5, n_points)\n   - Efficiency measurement: Count of gradient uses in -2.0 to -0.2 normalized space\n\nThe program implements a joint continuous-discrete optimization environment with repair-phase longevity preservation. Bayesian thrust is steered by gradient-focused exploration kernels, with repair maintaining search continuity. User Interaction points: None; environment fully self-contained.",
